WEBVTT

NOTE automatic transcription, uncorrected

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
When I first started at Cedric, it was a very small office, as it always had been.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.680
I think there was a secretary, two other people and me.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:16.207
And the reason that he took me on is that at that time he had just got the

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:16.880
InterAction

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.880
Centre as a project.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:22.640
Work had commenced on it, not too far.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.680
And I was a humble assistant.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.167
which I thought was a very good project. As it turned out, it was the last thing he

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:32.600
actually

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:38.843
ever built, but no one knew that at the time. And Cedric was very interesting in

00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:39.780
that because

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:47.353
Ed Berman, who was the client, who was an American but lived in London for a long

00:00:47.353 --> 00:00:47.820
time,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:54.013
and started off observing that there were... He came out of the theatre background

00:00:54.013 --> 00:00:54.240
and

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.560
observing a lot of kids not going to school, hanging around on the streets.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.580
So he started dressing up as a clown, pushing a street cart,

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.660
but the cart was full of toys and other games and other things.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.720
It's like the Pied Piper of Hamelin, a little bit.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.440
And then that was very popular and then he formalised it a bit and then more people

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.760
came in and the whole thing grew as a sort of

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.140
community action sort of group.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:20.600
And it got to the point where

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.600
it was very successful. It had a city farm,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:30.890
very early city farm, quite a common concept as you know, and they needed a new

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:31.720
building.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.260
And the borough of Camden here in London, they said well you can have this piece of

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:47.564
land via railway until 2002, otherwise more or less a 25 year period. So it was not

00:01:47.564 --> 00:01:47.700
a

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.169
lot of money but they raised a bit of money and that was interesting. So this was

00:01:52.169 --> 00:01:52.500
during

00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.490
a period of high inflation in the UK, very high inflation. So it was decided that,

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:00.880
this

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.158
is an important point because it suited Cedric perfectly, the money they had would

00:02:06.158 --> 00:02:06.480
buy a

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.938
drainage system, a concrete slab and a steel frame. It was better to spend that

00:02:11.938 --> 00:02:12.560
money now

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.452
because if you waited the price would go up significantly and therefore you would

00:02:16.452 --> 00:02:16.740
always

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.325
be chasing the money. So you might as well spend what you had and then there can be

00:02:21.325 --> 00:02:21.380
a

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:26.120
Of course, in effect, you had a one-to-one model of the building.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.840
And I often think, and I'm sure Cedric thought,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.460
that in a way it was more useful like that

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:35.200
than actually finishing the building.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.040
Because Ed's outfit was a bit anarchic, I have to say,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.520
and there was a high turnover of people.

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.900
So every two weeks, my job was to...

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.060
This was after the frame went up and everything.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:55.176
My job was to go there and have a conversation with all the people in the different

00:02:55.176 --> 00:02:56.060
departments.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.560
There was silkscreen printing, there was car maintenance, there was some physical

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:01.060
exercise.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.060
It was like a school and there was a pub in there.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:11.648
We'd actually chalk out various areas on the ground and say, yeah, that's what I

00:03:11.648 --> 00:03:12.060
want.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:16.060
A month later, it would be somebody else in charge of silkscreen printing.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.740
And you had different ideas.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:21.860
So nothing really ever resolved itself on the plan.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.580
And in the meantime, we were doing some of the details

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.180
in readiness for when they got the other money.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.280
When it came to the details,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.800
Cedric didn't have much to say about these things.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.380
I was not exactly experienced either.

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:50.676
by that time the more experienced assistant at Cedric he had moved on, I found

00:03:50.676 --> 00:03:51.160
myself

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:58.160
in charge of the project. And when I had a genuine question I'd say to Cedric in the

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:04.840
mornings, I've got an issue, he'd say I'll talk to you later about that, which meant

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.737
he never did talk to you about it, so you just had to sort of resolve it yourself.

00:04:08.737 --> 00:04:08.880
The

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.033
one thing that he did, there were two things that he said to me which I think were

00:04:13.033 --> 00:04:13.540
important.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:19.716
which was related to the detailing, that everything you should take apart from the

00:04:19.716 --> 00:04:20.620
steel frame,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.236
everything else in the building you should be able to take apart with a standard

00:04:25.236 --> 00:04:25.640
bicycle

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:33.676
spanner. I thought that was interesting. The other thing he said, never take a

00:04:33.676 --> 00:04:34.500
decision

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:42.021
until you have to. Which I think was really good, I still think about that today.

00:04:42.021 --> 00:04:42.300
Why

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.270
Why would you decide that you're going to do this when you don't have to take that

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:45.560
decision

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:51.340
because in a month it might look a bit different and then you do something else.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:52.340
I think that's true.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.540
It's one of the problems with getting planning permission.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.075
Everything is so fixed that actually as you go on and evolve the detail of the

00:05:00.075 --> 00:05:00.540
building

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:04.721
and you're actually in the process of building, it might be something much better to

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:05.020
do but

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.560
actually by law you're fixed to what you've already got permission for.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:17.320
I probably last went to that building in the early 21st century, 2001, 2002.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:20.800
Occasionally I like to go back, very occasionally.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:23.480
No one ever changed anything.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.480
And that was the reality.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.639
And that has to make you think, and of course if Cedric had still been around then,

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:28.940
surely

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:34.320
he'd be thinking, you know, the notion of flexibility, uncertainty, and uncertainty

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.236
underpinned much of his thinking related to architecture in general. You had to

00:05:40.236 --> 00:05:40.760
examine

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:47.427
that. You had to say, well, in the end, you don't need to move everything unless

00:05:47.427 --> 00:05:47.760
it's

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:54.620
a real emergency or a complete change in thought because people in themselves are

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.060
really adaptable.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:00.420
They can make anything out of anything. If I give you a couple of sheds put together

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.020
and a bit of a tent, you can live in it.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.760
It's not a house, but it is a home.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.500
And I think the same is true,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.320
particularly when it came to a community centre.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:13.800
However, the building itself was highly successful

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:16.020
and it was well used.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:19.840
Whether people liked it or not, I don't know.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.780
That didn't seem to be the point.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.940
They used it and it performed a useful purpose.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:26.960
But people of all ages, it wasn't just children,

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.820
It was all ages, because a lot of problems at that time in that part of London.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.760
Did Cedric have a real interest in the build?

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.400
I've often pondered that, because he still had plenty of active life as an architect

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:40.900
left in him at that time.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:46.000
He wasn't exactly short of contacts.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:53.727
He was very well connected, and opportunities to build did come his way, or

00:06:53.727 --> 00:06:54.640
requests.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.700
But he didn't do it.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:02.001
I mean, I think there was something, was it a greenhouse or something in Paris,

00:07:02.001 --> 00:07:05.020
which failed, I think, for financial reasons.

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:07.720
I don't really know the story because it was after my time with him.

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:11.300
I think he was genuinely disappointed not to build that.

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:14.480
I think he really wanted to build that because he found that interesting.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:17.200
But did he really want to build it?

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.800
And I've often pondered that question.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:23.800
I think that he didn't really.

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:31.490
He enjoyed the conversations, he enjoyed the discussion around the project, he

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:32.180
enjoyed

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:37.880
clearly the discussion around architecture and what it meant in general and he was a

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:41.403
very good critic which is why he was very popular to come to juries here and many

00:07:41.403 --> 00:07:41.620
other

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.700
places.

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:49.500
That's what he enjoyed and of course we all tend to pursue what we enjoy.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:59.504
He didn't enjoy minutes of meetings, the possible legal aspects of building, the

00:07:59.504 --> 00:08:00.880
minutiae of

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.880
the building regs.

00:08:03.880 --> 00:08:09.800
These are all things which I think he just didn't want to do that.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.682
The clue there was when I was asking him how to resolve a certain detail and I

00:08:13.682 --> 00:08:14.080
couldn't

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:15.080
see him for dust.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:16.080
He was off.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.522
He was very critical of the school, but he would also participate in the school at

00:08:20.522 --> 00:08:21.280
the same time.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.400
He was always very critical of Alvin.

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:29.220
Very rude to us, just around the corner, about Alvin.

00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:34.406
I thought, well, Alvin's not my best man in the world, but he's certainly not the

00:08:34.406 --> 00:08:34.780
worst.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.398
And I didn't understand that inevitably, without Alvin, this place would have

00:08:39.398 --> 00:08:39.740
closed.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:41.900
There's no doubt about it.

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:46.100
and he saved the school and it went on and did what it did.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:49.960
Which is strange. So I think that Elvin,

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:53.620
sorry, I think that Cedric sometimes

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:58.000
deliberately set up people not as enemies but as

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.940
they were, they were, he was like

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.400
practicing in his own head, I think,

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.460
what he really thought about the education here at the school

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:11.160
and what he was doing. He had no intention of doing anything about it.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:15.894
and he would come here and give the odd lecture and things but um and yeah i think

00:09:15.894 --> 00:09:16.760
he's very happy

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:21.240
to do that sometimes students would go around and see him upstairs because obviously

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:21.560
in the

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:27.789
upstairs around the corner i say upstairs because he had the room at the top of his

00:09:27.789 --> 00:09:28.540
office his

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:36.313
office is on one two three floors first second and fourth floor and the top floor

00:09:36.313 --> 00:09:37.560
was the white

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:43.140
room or also known as East Grinstead. It was called East Grinstead so that if

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:43.720
somebody

00:09:43.720 --> 00:09:49.420
called for him and the secretary said, I'm sorry, he's in East Grinstead, she wasn't

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:53.265
actually telling a lie, but actually he was upstairs, but there was no phone link up

00:09:53.265 --> 00:09:53.540
there.

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:59.377
He would disappear up there for a long time with his sketchbook, the newspapers, the

00:09:59.377 --> 00:09:59.760
brandy

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.506
and the cigar. The McAlpines were worried, the organisation, that they had to do

00:10:04.506 --> 00:10:05.040
something

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.374
about the conditions of building sites. Otherwise it becomes an unattractive job and

00:10:10.374 --> 00:10:11.200
they wouldn't

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:17.140
be able to get the employees to work, particularly in central London sites. So by

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:18.020
that time if

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:23.625
you were working in Mayfair on site, let's say people working on building sites at

00:10:23.625 --> 00:10:24.240
lunchtime

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.367
would go and have a drink in the pub. It wouldn't happen today. They found that they

00:10:28.367 --> 00:10:28.760
were not

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:34.129
allowed to go into the pub because of the way that they were dressed. So we looked

00:10:34.129 --> 00:10:34.260
at

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:38.784
all the aspects of the clothes, ripping off stuff so you could actually look

00:10:38.784 --> 00:10:39.260
alright,

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:42.260
so you could go to the pub if you wanted to.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:51.260
It was really the beginning of blue jeans in terms of as part of your working outfit

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:55.260
and with a lot of talking to experts.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:11:01.312
Young men working on building sites in the cult in blue jeans would actually suffer

00:11:01.312 --> 00:11:02.260
later in life

00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:07.260
arthritis and other things because it's not the right clothing to wear.

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:12.708
When I first started at Cedric, the office hours were 10 o'clock in the morning

00:11:12.708 --> 00:11:13.260
until 6.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:20.260
And that suited me just fine. Not too early.

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:29.260
And that worked well. Very often you'd arrive in the morning

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.100
and just sit down and get on with whatever you were doing.

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:39.480
And Cedric, he had a small compartment on the main floor,

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.600
on the second floor, where reception also was.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:46.620
And he would sit there and he would take phone calls there.

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.920
So he would do all his phone calls in the morning

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.960
and then when he finished those phone calls,

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:54.440
he would go upstairs to the white room or East Gwynstead.

00:11:54.440 --> 00:12:01.221
And he would then, but it was in that gap between phone calls and going upstairs

00:12:01.221 --> 00:12:01.560
that

00:12:01.560 --> 00:12:02.740
his son talked to you.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:06.420
Not necessarily about what you were doing, but anything that he was thinking.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:12.900
And we discussed things that were in journals and other aspects.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.900
And he was a great one for anecdotes.

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:21.407
But of course every man particularly, more than women, as they get older they like

00:12:21.407 --> 00:12:22.200
anecdotes.

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:26.840
because actually it's good. I find I do it myself with my own employees.

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:32.900
And because it's a way of learning, it's a way of sort of passing on things.

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:38.720
When we got to 1976, which was a famously very hot summer here in London,

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:44.380
his office had large windows. It wasn't very big, facing east and south.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:46.860
No air conditioning, of course.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:50.885
And by the time it got to two o'clock in the afternoon, you couldn't work in the

00:12:50.885 --> 00:12:51.200
office.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:57.264
I mean it was just unbearable. So I had this idea, I said look, why don't we start

00:12:57.264 --> 00:12:57.560
at 7

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:03.021
o'clock in the morning and just work for seven hours and go home at 2, because it

00:13:03.021 --> 00:13:03.560
would be

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:07.652
better. He loved that because it was playing with time. He was always interested in

00:13:07.652 --> 00:13:07.800
the

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:14.481
concept of time and therefore you're playing with time in the context of the office.

00:13:14.481 --> 00:13:14.720
And

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:19.699
that's what we did. It was a real struggle getting there at 7, but you get used to

00:13:19.699 --> 00:13:19.820
it

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:24.010
after a while. And it clearly was a struggle for the Secretary to get there at 7,

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:24.320
but he

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:29.769
wanted to be there before you, to make a point. It always amused me. So he'd arrive

00:13:29.769 --> 00:13:30.360
and there

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.220
he was, but he was out of breath, he'd just run up the stairs.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:39.893
What was the Hot Stuff Club? It was an excuse to drink, I think, and it involved

00:13:39.893 --> 00:13:40.560
Patricia

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:46.360
Dawkins, who was the Secretary for quite a long time, who was a very nice girl, lots

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:52.636
of fun and I think the Hot Stuff Club involved certainly Frank Newby and David

00:13:52.636 --> 00:13:53.360
Alford. I

00:13:55.860 --> 00:14:02.322
think the then leader of the Trade Union Congress was also a part of it and they had

00:14:02.322 --> 00:14:02.860
to have

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:10.298
a woman and that was Patricia. Us young men were kept well away from the Hot Stuff

00:14:10.298 --> 00:14:10.700
Club.

00:14:10.700 --> 00:14:19.840
from the Hot Stuff Club. But just to go back to Trode, brother, and the White Room,

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:20.280
when

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:26.001
there was celebration, I mean he loved birthdays and any reason to celebrate

00:14:26.001 --> 00:14:26.980
something. Or

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:33.919
it might be the 21st birthday of the office, I think that might have been the Smarty

00:14:33.919 --> 00:14:34.580
incident

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:40.951
in the White Room. So it was a big party and the umbrellas which were filled with

00:14:40.951 --> 00:14:41.580
Smarties

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:48.688
and you opened them and they fell everywhere. I can't remember much else but Trode

00:14:48.688 --> 00:14:48.960
also

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:54.601
loved board games and there was a fairly new board game at the time called Escape

00:14:54.601 --> 00:14:54.880
from

00:14:54.880 --> 00:15:01.880
Cold Dits and we'd have evenings where we would play this and Trode would actually,

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:07.680
But actually all the food was all tied up in brown paper and bits of string.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:10.000
And he always had to be the Nazi.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.360
Because I think he had a helmet and jackboots.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:16.200
And you had to have the Nazi.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:19.564
And everyone else was sort of dressed up in old clothes with bits of string around

00:15:19.564 --> 00:15:19.680
you

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.060
as though you were escaping from a prison camp.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:25.560
And then there was lots of drink.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:30.760
But then he had to drink the wine out of old tin mugs and everything.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:37.740
It was all nicely done, just under one low watt light bulb that was bare.

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:39.480
It was a horrible light.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.460
It was all about effect and having fun.

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:46.880
It was about removing yourself from all the other stuff you were doing in the day.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:48.620
It was an alternative life.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:52.960
In some ways, I think the White Room was an alternative life.

00:15:53.460 --> 00:15:54.940
It was certainly a retreat.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:57.940
It was a place to think about other things and to do other things.

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:02.642
And of course, I'm sure if Cedric was here and you asked him what was the White

00:16:02.642 --> 00:16:02.940
Room,

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:08.940
that's where I did my work. But he did much more than that up there, much more.

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:15.468
You can understand the statement as to the role of the architect as being to enable

00:16:15.468 --> 00:16:15.940
things

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:22.224
in a number of practical ways. I mean, he always said that if somebody asked an

00:16:22.224 --> 00:16:22.940
architect

00:16:22.940 --> 00:16:28.558
design a new house, maybe it's a divorce that's required. So it's always looking

00:16:28.558 --> 00:16:29.260
behind the

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:33.938
reasons as to why you think you need a new building. So if there's any way of not

00:16:33.938 --> 00:16:34.400
building

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:39.437
a building and solving this problem. So it's very much, I mean, at that time when I

00:16:39.437 --> 00:16:39.680
came

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:45.051
into that, when I was a student here, people like Karl Popper were very influential

00:16:45.051 --> 00:16:45.180
in

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:50.500
the whole idea of problem solving. But of course, later in life, and I came to this

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:57.757
conclusion as well. You think, well, you can't characterize architecture as simply

00:16:57.757 --> 00:16:58.200
being

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:04.258
problem solving because lots of other people can solve problems. Architecture is

00:17:04.258 --> 00:17:04.940
certainly

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:11.700
something else. Don't ask me what. Don't, you dare. But there is something else and

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:17.415
that's why it's not always about solving problems. But at that time, Cedric would

00:17:17.415 --> 00:17:18.120
have said,

00:17:18.120 --> 00:17:21.689
Every day in the newspaper there is a series of things that architects could

00:17:21.689 --> 00:17:22.440
address, because

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:27.840
there are problems if you read a news article in a particular way.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:33.660
There's an architectural solution, but architecture in the widest sense of the word.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:39.826
So the role as an enabler was certainly a part of his vocabulary and part of his

00:17:39.826 --> 00:17:40.520
attitude.

00:17:40.520 --> 00:17:47.076
I think to a certain extent Cedric's major preoccupation sounds cruel, but it's

00:17:47.076 --> 00:17:47.740
actually

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:54.740
quite good, was himself, I have to say. I think he was very aware of who he was, his

00:17:55.600 --> 00:18:01.521
background and how respected he was, but all being it, albeit, it was a very

00:18:01.521 --> 00:18:02.300
particular

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:08.312
taste which didn't suit everyone. You can't imagine Cedric Price having a

00:18:08.312 --> 00:18:09.300
conversation

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:16.383
with Richard Meyer, can you, because it was chalk and cheese. I think that I often

00:18:16.383 --> 00:18:16.860
wonder

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:22.740
if he, with Joan Littlewood, had actually built the Fun Palace,

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:27.100
what that might have changed in his life.

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:31.740
I think he was very disappointed in particular that that project never occurred.

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.460
Because again, it was the perfect project for him,

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.660
and she was the perfect client as well,

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:40.300
because she was completely mad, in a good way.

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:44.200
She was very inventive, she was trying to invent what theatre was

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:47.140
and how theatre was delivered to the general public

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:51.157
and what the role of the public was and the audience was and different ways of doing

00:18:51.157 --> 00:18:51.300
it.

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.457
Which was perfect for Cedric because that's what he's trying to do with architecture

00:18:55.457 --> 00:18:56.100
and building.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:02.443
And everything was a question mark and unresolved. I think that lack of resolution

00:19:02.443 --> 00:19:03.540
was an important

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:11.692
part of the work. Had he built it, of course, I don't know. I think he was, and I

00:19:11.692 --> 00:19:12.900
get angry as

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:18.900
well you know that the Santa Pompidou would not be as it was without without

00:19:18.900 --> 00:19:23.860
the fun pass and I get angry about that not because I mean it's what one thing

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:27.680
leads to another we don't own any ideas you know once you've talked about an

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:34.860
idea it's belongs to the world but Richard Rogers and piano have never

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:39.300
really acknowledged Cedric public as far as I know I've never heard them make any

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:44.245
reference. I can never imagine how they could do that without referring to

00:19:44.245 --> 00:19:45.180
precedents. It

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:51.122
didn't come out of nowhere, for example. So I think that Cedric was quite

00:19:51.122 --> 00:19:52.180
disappointed,

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:55.755
quite offended by that, that he had done a lot of groundwork. It was very

00:19:55.755 --> 00:19:56.240
interesting

00:19:56.240 --> 00:20:02.670
at the time. That's why it was well published and well known by a wide range of

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:03.240
people.

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:08.314
And then the Popadou Center comes. Well, I mean, that's it. It's not it. I mean, it

00:20:08.314 --> 00:20:08.620
would

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:12.254
have been very different had Cedric been doing but there was an aesthetic based in

00:20:12.254 --> 00:20:12.520
there.

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:17.672
I mean I think that there were other things going on at the time which you had to,

00:20:17.672 --> 00:20:17.860
all

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:23.266
the test rigs for the vertical takeoff aircraft, it's called the flying bedstead,

00:20:23.266 --> 00:20:24.000
looked like

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:30.836
the Fun Palace. I mean really did. But that's the whole thing, it's a sort of test

00:20:30.836 --> 00:20:31.420
bed for

00:20:31.420 --> 00:20:36.666
trying something out and it's exactly the same philosophy with buildings in a way

00:20:36.666 --> 00:20:36.860
the

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:41.714
InterAction Centre which I've already discussed, in a way was a test bed for trying

00:20:41.714 --> 00:20:42.240
something

00:20:42.240 --> 00:20:48.696
out. The problem there was it really wasn't the right people with the right kind

00:20:48.696 --> 00:20:49.100
of...

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:54.213
they had lots of energy but not energy for that in the actual building. Did that

00:20:54.213 --> 00:20:54.660
matter?

00:20:54.660 --> 00:21:04.840
Not really. I think the McAfee project was also a perfect project for Cedric. It was

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:12.911
about making life better for a particular set of people in terms of food, clothes,

00:21:12.911 --> 00:21:13.600
safety,

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:17.780
health and safety. He was very quickly on to health and safety. I don't know what he

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:24.051
thought later in life when health and safety had become a menace, but nonetheless,

00:21:24.051 --> 00:21:24.280
the

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:28.753
idea of saving life, having conditions, it's actually good for humanity. I think

00:21:28.753 --> 00:21:29.200
that was

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:36.914
important to him. In a way Cedric's role is a sort of conscience really,

00:21:36.914 --> 00:21:38.200
particularly

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:44.662
to other architects, maybe to others as well. Often when I'm thinking about a new

00:21:44.662 --> 00:21:45.300
project,

00:21:45.300 --> 00:21:49.133
fiddling around and scratching my head, sometimes I feel Cedric sitting on my

00:21:49.133 --> 00:21:49.780
shoulder like

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:56.048
some sort of parrot and says, don't do that. How dare you think that. In a way I

00:21:56.048 --> 00:21:56.440
think

00:21:56.440 --> 00:22:03.033
quite a good role, I'm very happy with that, very thankful for it. And for a long

00:22:03.033 --> 00:22:03.440
time,

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:09.925
you know, I've been teaching in Vienna for quite a while, you know, I occasionally

00:22:09.925 --> 00:22:10.320
bring

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:19.547
up Cedric in conversation. A whole body of students never heard of him, had no idea

00:22:19.547 --> 00:22:19.880
who

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:24.728
Cedric Price was, which I found a bit upsetting. I found in the last five to six

00:22:24.728 --> 00:22:25.880
years in particular

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:29.880
Now students come up to me and say, you used to work for Cedric.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:33.880
And they wanted a conversation. In much the same way as they're very aware of

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.880
Archigram. Archigram went completely out of fashion I suppose

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.880
in some way, or out of the consciousness of the younger architect.

00:22:41.880 --> 00:22:45.880
They're now back in and I think that

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.880
I always take that, which would be a certain compliment

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:53.880
to Cedric, is that the more interesting students

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:59.920
gone beyond the idea of style. Where it's going is a whole other film that we might

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:06.453
make in the future. But I think that's good. There's no assumptions, really. I think

00:23:06.453 --> 00:23:06.920
that's

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:09.420
a good attitude to maintain.
